==English==

===Etymology===
Late 16th century, from earlier {{term|heyda|lang=en}} (1520s), as exclamation – compare {{term|hey|lang=en}}, {{term|hei|lang=enm}}. Sense “period of success, vigor” from 1751, which respelt as '''heyday''' based on unrelated {{term|day|lang=en}} (as “period of time”) – compare {{term|day in the sun|lang=en}}.<ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-us-heyday.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|eɪdeɪ|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[period]] of [[success]], [[popularity]], or [[power]]; [[prime]].
#: ''The early twentieth century was the '''heyday''' of the steam locomotive.''

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|day in the sun}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|en|hey}}

